% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 6
% composition-elements: 9/8 metre grouped two ways, 3+3+3 in the A sections and 2+2+2+3 in the B section; layering, a melody over an instrumental ostinato that marks each grouping; I-IV-V harmony (D, G, A); ABA' form ending on a held tonic
% composition-listen: Zakir Hussain — "Making Music" (composed by Zakir Hussain), title track of the album Making Music with Hariprasad Chaurasia, John McLaughlin and Jan Garbarek (ECM 1349; recorded December 1986, Rainbow Studio, Oslo; released 1987)
% composition-listen-url: https://ecmrecords.com/product/making-music-zakir-hussain/
% composition-bridge: Hear rhythmic ideas stated, varied and answered across instruments while a larger cycle remains intelligible.
% composition-context: Zakir Hussain's collaborations can open discussion of tabla, Hindustani rhythmic training and intercultural ensemble work. This original 9/8 carousel is not a tala composition.
% composition-distance: Original melody and neutral classroom rhythm; no tabla composition, bol sequence or recorded passage is copied.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"5-8","role":"clapping group","prompt":"Group nine eighths","choices":["2+2+2+3 with the tune","3+3+3 against it","4+2+3"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"9-12","role":"conductor","prompt":"Change the density","choices":["melody alone","melody and ostinato","everyone, then melody alone at the end"],"replyBars":4}
% dossier-function: original instrumental grouping piece for Grade 6: a melody group and an ostinato group show the same nine eighths grouped 3+3+3 and then 2+2+2+3, while a clapping group chooses a grouping and a conductor changes the density on the return
% dossier-elements: twelve bars in 9/8 (nine eighths); D major, melody pitch set D-E-F#-G-A-B-C#-D-E, range D4-E5; ostinato G3-E4 on chord roots and fifths; A (bars 1-4) in three dotted-quarter groups, the melody moving quarter-eighth, quarter-eighth, dotted quarter; B (bars 5-8) in 2+2+2+3, three quarters and a dotted quarter in every bar, with the ostinato in the same grouping; A' (bars 9-12) returns and closes on D4 held for the full bar of nine eighths; harmony D, G-A, D, A-D | G, D, G-A, A | D, G-A, A, D (I-IV-V); ABA' form
% dossier-practice: Dotted quarter = 80 (eighth = 240) is the target; begin at eighth = 180 and count eighths aloud, "1 2 3 1 2 3 1 2 3" for A and "1 2 1 2 1 2 1 2 3" for B. Rehearsal key D major; the melody's written range D4-E5 suits recorder, xylophone or glockenspiel and lies inside the young-unison C4-D5 range except the one E5 in bar 3; the ostinato G3-E4 suits bass xylophone, low bells, cello or keyboard. Teach in steps: clap and count both groupings; learn the A melody; learn the B melody; learn the ostinato; layer them; add the clapping choice in B and the conductor's density choice on the return. Body percussion (pat on the low notes, clap on the others) may replace the ostinato's pitches.
% dossier-accompaniment: The ostinato (second voice) and the D-G-A chords are newly authored. The page's folk band was chosen as a plain, non-specific accompaniment; it is not tabla and not a tala, and the page's drone style (tanpura and tabla) is deliberately not used. The band's groove may not follow the 2+2+2+3 grouping of bars 5-8; mute its drums there if it blurs the grouping and let the class ostinato carry it.
% dossier-lyrics: Instrumental piece; no words. Counting syllables are plain numbers, so that no invented syllables are presented as tabla bols or konnakol.
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed range, metre and teaching constraints. Not traditional, not collected from any community, not a tala, a tabla composition or a bol sequence, and not modelled on the reference's melody or rhythm; the Zakir Hussain recording is a listening bridge only.
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0 for the melody, ostinato, chords and this ABC encoding. "Making Music" (Zakir Hussain) and its ECM recording remain their owners' copyright and are not included.
% dossier-respect: Name Zakir Hussain (1951-2024) as a tabla master, son and student of Ustad Alla Rakha, and name his collaborators on the record. Use a tabla educator's demonstration for bols, tala and technique; do not call arbitrary syllables konnakol or tabla language, and never present this classroom 9/8 piece as a tala or as Hindustani music.
% dossier-review: Grade 6 classroom music teacher, tabla or Hindustani rhythm educator, second score reader and student playing trial; all reviews still required
