N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit, every bar nine eighths: A, bar 1 quarter-eighth, quarter-eighth, dotted quarter (D F# A B D5); bar 2 the same rhythm (D5 B G B A, G then A chord); bar 3 the same rhythm (F# A D5 E5 D5); bar 4 the same rhythm (C#5 B A E D, A then D chord). B, bars 5-8 three quarters and a dotted quarter each (G B D5 B; A F# D F#; B D5 B C#5; C#5 A E A). A', bars 9-10 repeat bars 1-2, bar 11 C#5 B A F# E in the A-section rhythm, bar 12 D4 held for the whole bar. Ostinato: three dotted quarters (root, fifth, fifth, or the next chord's root) in the A sections, and quarter, quarter, quarter, dotted quarter in B, ending on a full-bar D4. Melody pitch set D-E-F#-G-A-B-C#-D-E, range D4-E5, largest leap the fifth A4 down to D4 into bar 9, with thirds and fourths elsewhere (A4-E4 in bar 8); the analyzer reads the quarter notes that cross the dotted-quarter beat in bars 5-8 as syncopation, which here is the 2+2+2+3 grouping; ostinato G3-E4. Form ABA'. Originality check: the D-F#-A-B-D rise and the 2+2+2+3 arpeggios were compared with what the reference's opening exposes and with common jig, slip-jig and children's tunes; no shared phrase was found; triad arpeggios in 9/8 and the 2+2+2+3 grouping itself are common property.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 6 players switch cleanly from 3+3+3 to 2+2+2+3 at eighth = 240, whether the ostinato group holds its grouping under the melody, and how the page's folk band plays 9/8 (and whether it fights the 2+2+2+3 bars) can only be settled by a classroom trial. A tabla educator should judge whether the listening framing is accurate and respectful. The ECM page is the label's official page for the album; it names the tracks but does not itself play the title track.
